# Canonical URL form is extensionless: /licensing, never /licensing.html
#
# Netlify already serves licensing.html at /licensing by itself, so the pretty
# form needs no rule here — the old "/licensing → /licensing.html 301" family
# was both a no-op (an existing file wins over a redirect rule) and pointed the
# wrong way, putting .html back in the address bar for the slugs whose name
# didn't match their file. The rules below retire the .html form instead.
#
# The trailing "!" is load-bearing, and for the same reason the old rules were
# dead: /licensing.html IS a real file, so an unforced rule never fires. The
# force flag is what lets the redirect win over the file. Drop the "!" and
# these silently become no-ops that still LOOK correct in this file.
/311.html                   /311                     301!
/ai.html                    /ai                      301!
/budget-management.html     /budget-management        301!
/human-resources.html       /human-resources          301!
/land-management.html       /land-management          301!
/licensing.html             /licensing                301!
/privacy-policy.html        /privacy-policy           301!
/record-requests.html       /record-requests          301!
/reports.html               /reports                  301!
/terms-of-use.html          /terms-of-use             301!
/thanks.html                /thanks                   301!
/index.html                 /                         301!

# contacts.html is deliberately NOT redirected. Its demo-request form POSTs to
# its own URL, and browsers downgrade a 301'd POST to GET — which would drop
# submissions on the floor, the same class of breakage as the catch-all rule
# removed from netlify.toml. contacts.html carries a rel="canonical" instead.

# Section shortcuts
/home          /                        200
/solutions     /#modules                302
/approach      /#approach               302

# Old Webflow slugs → canonical clean paths (active at cutover)
/citizen-service-requests   /311                     301
/record-request             /record-requests          301
/reports-and-dashboards     /reports                  301
/contact                    /contacts                 301

# Pages that exist on the old site but NOT yet on new (defer until rebuild):
# /about, /product, /articles/* — temporary 302 to homepage
/about         /             302
/product       /             302
/articles/*    /             302

